          "session_id": "full-mt-m-012",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My cholesterol came back at 6.4.",
          "answer": "I couldn't find a total cholesterol cutoff in the sources available to me, so I can't say whether 6.4 mmol/L is high, borderline or normal. The guidance I found does not classify total cholesterol by number alone.\n\nInstead, it estimates a person's 10-year risk of heart and blood vessel disease (source: *Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation*). That estimate uses age, sex, total and HDL cholesterol, blood pressure, diabetes, smoking status, and whether blood pressure is treated. For adults aged 40 to 75 without existing heart disease, the risk bands are:\n\n- **Low:** below 5% over 10 years\n- **Borderline:** 5% to below 7.5%\n- **Intermediate:** 7.5% to below 20%\n- **High:** 20% or above\n\nOther factors can shift a decision within the borderline and intermediate bands. These include family history of early heart disease, kidney disease, persistently raised triglycerides, and metabolic syndrome. The guidance also mentions a coronary artery calcium score for cases that remain unclear.\n\nYour clinician will usually look at your full lipid panel (including HDL, LDL and triglycerides), your blood pressure, blood sugar, smoking status and family history. Together these give your risk band. The next steps depend on that band and are for you and your clinician to decide. Ask them what your estimated 10-year risk is and what it means for you.\n\nI didn't find cholesterol-specific diet or activity guidance in the sources available to me. The lifestyle passages I retrieved covered blood pressure, heart failure, diabetes and reflux, so I haven't applied them here.\n\nIf you have chest pain, pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back, sudden breathlessness, or fainting, seek emergency care now. Do not wait for a routine appointment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
